## Plugin authoring

If the user asks for a package manager no official plugin covers (`pacman`,
`apk`, `flatpak`, a custom internal tool), scaffold a local plugin with
`homeos plugin add --local <name>`. That writes `plugins/<name>/plugin.yml`
and a full set of template files (`install.sh.tmpl`, etc.) for you or the
user to fill in.

The mechanics are a parameter list in `plugin.yml` plus templates using
`{{ key }}` substitution. The README's "Plugin Development Guide" is the
authoritative source — read it rather than improvising, since the plugin
schema changes between versions.

## Maintaining the repository README

The data directory is a Git repository, and like any repo it benefits from a
root `README.md` (`<data_dir>/README.md`) explaining what it manages. This
README is **for humans** — the user, future contributors, anyone who clones
the repo onto a new machine.

`homeos` does not generate it; the definition lives here in `AGENTS.md` and
you (the agent) maintain it, so the conventions evolve with whichever homeos
version is installed rather than leaving a stale skeleton from an older
release.

### When to create it

On the first mutation (plugin add, package add, etc.) in a session
where `<data_dir>/README.md` does not exist, create it using the
structure below. Include it in the first commit. The title is the
fixed string `homeos-repo` — do not prompt the user, do not personalize
with the hostname (this file gets pushed to remote, hostname leakage
should be avoided).

### When to update it

Whenever you mutate `homeos.yml`, refresh the corresponding section of
the README in the same commit as the mutation. The user does not need
to remember to update it — that is part of your job.

### When the conventions have changed

These conventions travel with the installed homeos version, so a README from
an older release — or one a previous session shaped differently — can drift
from what's described here. Routine cell updates you make in stride, but
reshaping the file's structure mid-session is surprising unannounced: when
you notice the README no longer matches these conventions, say what's out of
step and propose the realignment, then apply it once the user's on board.
The **Notes** section is never restructured — it belongs to the user.

### Structure

```markdown
# homeos-repo

This repository is the source of truth for the user's software setup,
managed by [homeos](https://github.com/hainet50b/homeos). The full
machine-readable definition lives in [`homeos.yml`](homeos.yml); the
sections below are a human-friendly view kept in sync with it.

To restore on any machine:

​```sh
homeos init <this repo's URL>
homeos apply
​```

## Packages

| Package | Plugin | Dependencies | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| [claude](packages/claude/) | — | [bubblewrap](packages/bubblewrap/), [socat](packages/socat/) | Claude Code CLI. |
| [docker](packages/docker/) (disabled) | — | — | Container runtime. |
| [neovim](packages/neovim/) | [dnf](plugins/dnf/) | — | Primary text editor. |

## Skills

| Skill | Plugin | Dependencies | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| [frontend-design](packages/frontend-design/) | [gh-skill](plugins/gh-skill/) | — | Visual design guidance for building UI. |

## Plugins

| Plugin | Source | Description |
|---|---|---|
| [dnf](plugins/dnf/) | [hainet50b/homeos-plugin-dnf](https://github.com/hainet50b/homeos-plugin-dnf) | DNF package manager plugin for homeos. |
| [my-custom](plugins/my-custom/) | (local) | Hand-written plugin for X. |

## Notes

(free-form section for your own notes — homeos and the agent never modify it.)

---

*Managed by [homeos](https://github.com/hainet50b/homeos).*
```

### Filling in the tables

**Packages table** — one row per entry in `homeos.yml`'s `packages` whose
`archived` is not `true`. Archived entries are machine-reconciliation
tombstones, not part of the setup a human reader is looking at, so they get
no row at all; `homeos package list` (its `Status` column reports `enabled` /
`disabled` / `archived`) and `homeos package info` (its `Archived:` line) are
where to look for them.

- *Package*: link the name to `packages/<name>/` (relative path). Suffix
  with ` (disabled)` when the package's `enabled` is `false`.
- *Plugin*: if set, link to `plugins/<plugin>/`. Otherwise use `—`.
- *Dependencies*: comma-separated list from `depends_on`, each name
  linked to `packages/<name>/` like the *Package* column (dependencies
  are always packages in `homeos.yml`, so the link target exists), or
  `—`.
- *Purpose*: one short sentence describing the package itself — what the
  tool is, what it does, why the user keeps it. Keep relationship facts
  out of this cell: dependencies, dependents, install order, and script
  aliases belong in the *Dependencies* column or are implied by the
  table. "Primary text editor." — not "Installed before the claude
  package." Likewise, prefer what the package is over how it came to be
  added: the conversation that led to it (the request, comparisons
  weighed, machine-setup context) belongs in the commit message, not
  here — it reads as noise once the session is forgotten. "Markdown
  note-taking app." — not "Added while migrating notes from Obsidian as
  discussed."

Order the rows alphabetically by package name — the same order
`homeos.yml` uses. Do not group rows by theme or concern: what "belongs
together" shifts with environment and context, so grouped tables get
reshaped from session to session and end up harder to scan than a
predictable A-Z list. Alphabetical order is stable — a small edit never
reshuffles the table or churns the diff — and lets the reader find a
package by name. Keep it one table with no extra headings.

**Skills table** — same columns and same rules as the Packages table, for
the packages that install an **agent skill** rather than software. A skill is
a directory of instructions an AI agent loads on demand; a package that puts
one in place belongs here, and every other package stays in Packages. Judge by
what the package installs, not by which plugin backs it or how it is named —
`homeos.yml` draws no such distinction, so this split exists in the README and
nowhere else.

Omit either section — heading and table together — while it has no rows. A
repository with no skills reads exactly as one written before the split
existed, and the heading arrives with the first skill.

**Plugins table** — one row per entry in `homeos.yml`'s `plugins`:

- *Plugin*: link the name to `plugins/<name>/`.
- *Source*: the `url` from `homeos.yml`'s `plugins.<name>.url`, rendered
  as a Markdown link with the text `<owner>/<repo>`. For a local plugin
  (no URL), use `(local)`.
- *Description*: the `description` from `plugins/<name>/plugin.yml`.

**Notes** — leave it alone. This section belongs to the user.

### Ownership boundary

You own the **Packages**, **Skills**, and **Plugins** sections; keep them
in sync with `homeos.yml`. The **Notes** section is the user's: read it
for context, but do not edit or reorder its content. Do not rename or
restructure the file.

If the user manually edits the Packages, Skills, or Plugins sections, your next
update will overwrite their edits. When you first create the README,
mention this to the user and suggest they put custom prose in
**Notes**, which is preserved.

## Local customizations

The user may keep an `AGENTS.local.md` file at `<data_dir>/AGENTS.local.md`.
If it exists, read it after this guide and treat its
instructions as additional guidance specific to the user's setup — naming
conventions for their packages, preferred plugins, sensitive operations to
double-check, machines they're explicitly opted out of, and so on. The
instructions in `AGENTS.local.md` extend the ones here; they do not contradict
the safety rules (still always dry-run before mutating, still always commit
after a change).

`homeos` never reads, modifies, or removes `AGENTS.local.md`. It is the
user's file, hand-written. Treat its absence as "no extra instructions";
treat its presence as "read this carefully before acting".
